# Target C: star-free outer-train witness cells absorb the split-star branch

Date: 2026-10-02  
Base main: 75aac1464f80ca91256e96dc70ca0c865e0b3b0e  
Status: proof-only candidate. Level I and C-II remain open.

## 1. Result

This note removes the common-star hypothesis from the October 1 local witness-cell
theorem.

Let \(\tau\) be any non-singleton global outer-to-outer stopped train. Let its
Section-157 Jordan shore split be

\[
V=S\sqcup T,
\]

orient every crossing actual diameter vector from \(T\) into \(S\), and let \(u\)
be the oriented unit train direction. Then the crossing actual-diameter directions
form one contiguous block in the global oriented diameter-direction order.

Consequently exactly one of the following local alternatives holds.

1. No actual diameter is parallel to \(u\). Then the two global actual-diameter
   directions immediately bracketing \(u\) are crossing edges
   \(h_-,h_+\), they are consecutive global directions, and

   \[
   r_{h_-}\cdot Ju<0<r_{h_+}\cdot Ju .
   \tag{1}
   \]

2. The unique actual diameter \(h_0\) parallel to \(u\) exists. Then
   \(h_-,h_0,h_+\) are three consecutive global actual-diameter directions, all
   three cross \(\tau\), and the two outer rows satisfy (1).

Thus every O--O train has a canonical opposite-sign Section-157 witness cell in
the global diameter-direction order, without requiring the two witnesses to share
a diameter vertex.

For a family \(\mathcal L\) of O--O trains whose projective directions lie in a
real interval \(J\) of width \(0<W<\pi\), put

\[
a_D=\arcsin {1\over2D},
\qquad
N(W,D)=\left\lfloor {W\over2a_D}\right\rfloor+1,
\qquad
M(W,D)=2N(W,D)+1.
\tag{2}
\]

There is a subfamily

\[
\boxed{
|\mathcal L'|\ge {|\mathcal L|\over M(W,D)}
}
\tag{3}
\]

with one fixed global witness cell. In an open cell the same two consecutive
actual diameter edges \(h_-,h_+\) cross every retained train with opposite strict
transverse signs. In a threshold cell the same three consecutive edges
\(h_-,h_0,h_+\) cross every retained train, every retained train is parallel to
\(h_0\), and the outer two rows have opposite signs.

For a Section-90 phase shore,

\[
W_D={4K\over D},
\qquad 2a_D\ge {1\over D},
\]

so one may take the existing constant

\[
\boxed{
M_K=2\lfloor4K\rfloor+3.
}
\tag{4}
\]

The main application is immediate. Once the October 1 O--O rectangle theorem gives

\[
|A_0|,|B_0|
\ge
\left({\kappa^4\over8\pi^3}-o(1)\right)D,
\tag{5}
\]

apply (3) independently on the two Section-90 shores. There are subfamilies
\(A^\sharp\subseteq A_0\), \(B^\sharp\subseteq B_0\) such that every cross-pair
still crosses in an actual strict rhombus and

\[
\boxed{
|A^\sharp|,|B^\sharp|
\ge
\left(
{\kappa^4\over8\pi^3M_K}-o(1)
\right)D.
}
\tag{6}
\]

Each shore has one fixed star-free global diameter witness cell.

Therefore the previous O--O fork

\[
\text{linear split-star shore}
\quad\hbox{or}\quad
\text{fixed single-star rectangle}
\]

is unnecessary. Both cases enter the same fixed-witness-cell rectangle. In
particular the split-star O--O branch is no longer a separate multiplicity problem.

This is a branch reduction, not a C-II closure. The surviving witness-cell
mechanisms still include nonconstant open-cell phase, exact open-cell phase, and
exact threshold phase, as well as the independent inactive-interface branches.

## 2. Diameter endpoints are unique support extrema

Let \(F\) be the convex hull of the actual diameter endpoints. If \(ab\) is an
actual diameter and

\[
r=p_b-p_a,\qquad |r|=D,
\]

then for any other source point \(z\),

\[
|z-b|^2\le D^2=|r|^2
\]

gives

\[
(z-a)\cdot r\ge {|z-a|^2\over2}>0.
\tag{7}
\]

Similarly

\[
(z-b)\cdot r\le-{|z-b|^2\over2}<0.
\tag{8}
\]

Thus \(a\) is the unique minimum and \(b\) the unique maximum of the linear
functional \(x\mapsto r\cdot x\) on the source and on \(F\).

This is the same elementary normal-order fact used in the October 1 nested-bundle
theorem. It also reproves that every actual diameter endpoint is an extreme point.

Distinct actual diameter edges have distinct projective directions. Indeed two
parallel diameter segments coincide by audited Section 157.1.

## 3. A train shore gives two complementary hull intervals

Represent the O--O train by its proper Jordan crosscut in the outer-face disk, as
in audited Section 157. The crosscut divides the outer boundary into two arcs
corresponding to the shores \(T\) and \(S\).

Restrict those arcs to the extreme vertices of \(F\). They give two complementary
cyclic vertex intervals \(I_T,I_S\) in the convex order of \(F\). Repeated
outer-face occurrences may be resolved in the standard small regular neighborhood;
this does not change the cyclic order of the convex extreme vertices or which side
of the crosscut contains them.

Both intervals are nonempty because Section 157.3 supplies crossing actual
diameters.

Choose a line strictly separating the two finite vertex intervals. One convenient
construction is the line through relative interior points of the two transition
sides between \(I_T\) and \(I_S\). Let \(\phi\) be a normal pointing from \(I_T\)
toward \(I_S\). Then every segment joining \(I_T\) to \(I_S\), oriented from
\(T\) to \(S\), satisfies

\[
\phi\cdot r>0.
\tag{9}
\]

Hence all oriented crossing-diameter directions lie in one open semicircle and
admit one ordinary real angular lift of length less than \(\pi\).

## 4. Crossing diameter directions are a contiguous block

### Lemma 4.1 -- interval normal order

Let \(I,J\) be complementary nonempty cyclic vertex intervals of a convex polygon.
Suppose two actual diameter vectors \(r_1,r_2\), oriented from \(I\) to \(J\), lie
in one open angular semicircle. Every actual diameter direction occurring between
their two lifted directions in that semicircle also joins \(I\) to \(J\).

### Proof

As a support direction rotates, the unique minimizing vertex of a convex polygon
moves monotonically through cyclic vertex order. In particular, the directions whose
minimum support lies in one cyclic vertex interval form one cyclic angular interval;
the same is true for maximum support.

Let the lifted directions of \(r_1,r_2\) be
\(\theta_1<\theta_2<\theta_1+\pi\). Suppose, for contradiction, that while rotating
on the short arc from \(\theta_1\) to \(\theta_2\), the minimum support leaves \(I\)
and later returns to \(I\). Since \(I,J\) are complementary vertex intervals, the
intervening minimum-support interval for \(J\) would be trapped inside that short
arc, up to the two transition normals.

But reversing either diameter swaps its unique minimum and maximum endpoints.
Therefore the antipodal directions
\(\theta_1+\pi\) and \(\theta_2+\pi\) both have their unique minimum support in
\(J\). Neither lies on the short arc from \(\theta_1\) to \(\theta_2\). This
contradicts the fact that the minimum-\(J\) directions form one cyclic interval.
Hence the minimum support stays in \(I\) throughout the short arc.

Apply the same argument to the maximum support, or equivalently apply the minimum
argument after reversing every direction. The maximum support stays in \(J\)
throughout. By Section 2 actual diameter directions have unique extrema, so any
actual diameter direction between \(\theta_1\) and \(\theta_2\) joins \(I\) to
\(J\). QED.

Apply Lemma 4.1 with \(I=I_T\), \(J=I_S\). It follows that the actual diameter
directions crossing the train cut form one contiguous block in the global direction
order.

No common diameter vertex is used.

## 5. The KKT resultant locates the train direction inside that block

Audited Section 157.2 gives

\[
A_\tau u
=
\sum_{h\pitchfork\tau}\beta_h r_h,
\qquad
A_\tau>0,\quad \beta_h>0,
\tag{10}
\]

where every crossing vector is oriented from \(T\) into \(S\).

Dot (10) with the separator \(\phi\). Equation (9) gives

\[
\phi\cdot u>0,
\tag{11}
\]

so the orientation of \(u\) lies in the same real angular semicircle as all
crossing vectors.

Because a positive combination of vectors in an open semicircle lies in their
positive cone, the angle of \(u\) lies between the extreme crossing directions.
Audited Section 157.3 strengthens this to strict transverse bracing:

\[
\exists h_+,h_-\pitchfork\tau:
\qquad
r_{h_+}\cdot Ju>0,
\qquad
r_{h_-}\cdot Ju<0.
\tag{12}
\]

Thus there is at least one crossing direction on each strict side of \(u\).

If no actual diameter direction equals \(u\), take the immediate global predecessor
and successor of \(u\). They lie between crossing directions of opposite sign.
Section 4 makes them crossing edges. Since no global diameter direction lies between
them, they are consecutive and satisfy (1).

If one actual diameter direction equals \(u\), audited parallel-diameter uniqueness
makes it a unique edge \(h_0\). The immediate global predecessor and successor of
\(h_0\) again lie between crossing directions of opposite signs, hence are crossing
by Section 4. This gives the three-edge threshold cell.

This proves the star-free local witness-cell theorem.

## 6. Uniform extraction in a narrow phase interval

Let the distinct actual diameter directions in \(J\) be

\[
d_1<\cdots<d_N.
\]

Canonical shared/disjoint direction spacing implies every two consecutive global
diameter directions are separated by at least \(2a_D\). Hence

\[
N\le
\left\lfloor {W\over2a_D}\right\rfloor+1.
\tag{13}
\]

The \(N\) singleton threshold directions and the at most \(N+1\) open complementary
cells partition \(J\) into at most \(2N+1\le M(W,D)\) witness classes.

The local theorem shows that the witness cell of a train depends only on the cell
containing its direction, not on a chosen star. Pigeonholing proves (3).

In one retained open cell, its two boundary diameter edges are the same fixed
consecutive global edges for every train. They cross every retained train and have
opposite strict Section-157 signs.

In one retained threshold cell, all retained train directions are the same actual
diameter direction \(h_0\). The two adjacent global edges and \(h_0\) are fixed and
cross every retained train.

This proves the family theorem.

## 7. Split-star geometry becomes a special case, not a branch

Suppose a retained train is genuinely split-star: no one diameter vertex carries
both opposite-sign Section-157 witnesses.

In an open witness cell, the two canonical boundary witnesses \(h_-,h_+\) must be
vertex-disjoint. If they shared a vertex, that vertex itself would carry both strict
signs and would single-star brace the train.

The same conclusion holds for the outer witnesses of a threshold triple
\(h_-,h_0,h_+\): \(h_-\) and \(h_+\) are vertex-disjoint.

Hence after the family extraction, a linear split-star family is not a diffuse
collection of cut-dependent witness pairs. It has one fixed pair of vertex-disjoint
actual diameter edges crossing every retained train cut with opposite signs
(and, in the threshold case, one additional fixed parallel full-support diameter).

In the square-root matching regime this is compatible with the current Woodall
leaf/core theorem but much more localized than its raw \(O(\sqrt D)\)-carrier
conclusion. The Section-90 narrow-bin theorem gives the stronger constant number of
possible cells before any matching estimate is used.

## 8. The complete O--O rectangle can bypass star pigeonholing

The October 1 complete-rectangle theorem supplies (5). All directions on one
Section-90 shore lie in width \(4K/D\), so apply Section 6 independently to \(A_0\)
and \(B_0\). Completeness of the crossing rectangle is preserved under taking
subfamilies, giving (6).

This improves the previous fixed-star extraction in two ways.

First, it has no exceptional split-star alternative.

Second, it does not pay the old factor \(2C_K\) from first discarding a possible
linear split population and then pigeonholing single-star bracing vertices. The old
fixed-star/common-cell lower coefficient

\[
{\kappa^4\over16\pi^3 C_K M_K}
\]

is replaced at proof-only scope by

\[
\boxed{
c_{\rm UWC}:={\kappa^4\over8\pi^3 M_K}.
}
\tag{14}
\]

No numerical value of \(K\) or \(\kappa\) is needed for the theorem.

## 9. Downstream modules that no longer need a common star

The following October 1 arguments can be entered from the star-free rectangle
because their load-bearing hypotheses after extraction are the ordered O--O trains,
a common global witness cell, and fixed full-support witness rows, not a common
witness vertex.

1. The complete-grid rank-switching theorem is already stated for arbitrary ordered
   O--O trains and gives full rank, linearly many proper-support diameter rows, and
   linearly many support switches.

2. The witness-cell blocker dichotomy uses only the absence/presence of global
   diameter directions inside the common cell and the fact that its boundary witness
   rows are full-support. Thus distinct adjacent train directions are TB1, while an
   exact-direction plateau is TB2.

3. The arbitrary open-cell block transposition uses only the same fixed cell and
   full-support boundary rows. Therefore the nonconstant-phase star-free branch has
   the same TWO-SIGN versus inverse-\(D\) inactive-near-activity fork.

4. In exact phase, the gap-graph connectivity theorem is already a consequence of
   rank alone. If the common cell is a threshold cell, the fixed parallel row \(h_0\)
   supplied by the universal localization is full-support, so the paired-staircase
   proof has the same geometric input. If the common cell is open, the general
   nested-bundle theorem applies to nested exact-phase cuts and their KKT resultants.

Thus the old single-star/split-star distinction is not part of the live O--O
handoff after (6). The remaining distinctions are geometric witness-cell types.

This paragraph is a dependency composition of already proof-only October 1 modules;
it does not promote those modules or change theorem-state authority.

## 10. Scope and hostile checks

- The theorem uses the audited Section-157 Jordan shore split and positive KKT cut
  resultant.
- It uses only actual diameter endpoints and their convex normal order. No Euler
  trail or diameter-path inference is made.
- Parallel-diameter uniqueness is exactly audited Section 157.1.
- Contact-isolated source points are not deleted or inserted into a rigidity matrix.
- Repeated outer-face occurrences are handled by the same regular-neighborhood
  resolution used by the O--O crosscut stack.
- No individual KKT coefficient lower bound is used.
- A fixed pair of vertex-disjoint diameter rows may cross arbitrarily many nested
  cuts. The theorem does not claim otherwise.
- Constant-width/Reuleaux star regressions do not contradict the theorem; they lack
  the O--O contact-cut/KKT resultant hypotheses needed to select \(u\).
- The theorem does not exclude the resulting common-cell rectangle.
- Inactive-interface and macroscopic inactive-container branches are untouched.

## 11. New frontier

The O--O endpoint no longer needs a separate split-star multiplicity theorem.

Starting from a quadratic O--O crossing mass, the route is now

\[
\text{dense O--O crossings}
\Longrightarrow
\text{linear complete rectangle}
\Longrightarrow
\text{linear star-free fixed-witness-cell rectangle}
\Longrightarrow
\begin{cases}
\text{open-cell nonconstant phase},\\
\text{open-cell exact phase},\\
\text{threshold exact phase}.
\end{cases}
\tag{15}
\]

The highest-value next step is therefore not to bound the number of split-star
witness pairs. It is to attack the common-cell rectangle directly, with the stronger
coefficient \(c_{\rm UWC}\), using the rank-distributed proper diameter rows and the
current TB1/TB2 finite-move machinery.

Disposition: proof-only theorem and dependency reduction. No numerical search,
verifier execution, CI output, or unexecuted certificate code is used.
